Kent Paul Dolan schrieb:
> 
> The Shrewd Dude wrote:
> 
> > Monsters never loot your chests, so there's no need to impose
> > additional restrictions on your stash once you've placed it into a box.
> 
> Umm, no. Monsters, and even your own pets, pick up containers, and
> the containers can easily end up under some other objects, making
> finding them an ugly chore. Better by far to pin the container at a
> known
> point.

If the chest is heavy enough, your pets won't be able to lift it; if
you really _do_ stash, your stash chest will be heavy enough. The
monsters that can and will mess with a chest are to my knowledge only
gelatinous cubes (eats the chest and incorporates all items) and
nymphs (have no weight limit for picking up things). Hostile
gelatinous cubes are one of the main reasons to secure your stash with
Elbereth. If you turn up with a gelatinous cube pet and don't ditch or
change it immediately, you deserve what's coming to you ...

> > Also, carving the E-word into a square does not prevent a monster from
> > going there at some point; it just makes them run away from you if
> > you're standing on it.
> 
> Not so; monsters who respect Elbereth will not step on an _occupied_
> square with Elbereth, whether the occupant is the PC, of some object.
> I'm not sure about pets, though.

Pets will step on any old square they can get to, although traps and
cursed items are a strong repellant. AFAIK, non-hostiles will also not
avoid Elbereth squares. The ultimate safest stashing spot would thus
be:
-in a chest
-under a boulder
-on a burnt-in E-word
-with a cursed non-metallic item (e.g. cursed blank scroll) atop the
chest

I have three locations which often accumulate stashes, with hardly any
cropping up elsewhere: bottom level of Sokoban, Ludios, Valley of the
Dead. Stashable items are normally left in the dungeon or dragged to
the stairs, where they have to wait for the one big 'hoover run' to
collect everything worth collecting and pull it to one of the actual
stashes.

